<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theseus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theseus was a legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in Greek mythology, son of Aegeus, or of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, and of Aethra of Troezen. Myths credit him with killing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and with the synoecism, the union of the villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> under Athens. Classical Athenians honoured him as a founder of their state, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> brought his supposed bones home from Skyros.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theseus.jpg" alt="Theseus" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aison · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kylix_Theseus_Aison_MNA_Inv11365_n1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the myth Aegeus, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, was childless and consulted the Oracle of Delphi, whose cryptic reply he did not understand. His host <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittheus">Pittheus</a>, king of Troezen, understood it and gave him his daughter Aethra. Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>'s instructions in a dream, Aethra also waded to the island of Sphairia and was possessed there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, so that Theseus had both divine and mortal parentage. Before leaving, Aegeus buried his sandals and sword under a huge rock, to be taken by his son when he was strong enough to move it as proof of his royal birth.</p><p>Theseus was raised in Troezen. When he grew up he moved the rock and set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. He chose the dangerous land route round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf">Saronic Gulf</a> rather than the sea, and defeated a series of bandits on the way: Periphetes, Sinis, the Crommyonian Sow, Sciron, Cercyon and Procrustes, who was cut to fit his own beds. Scholars believe that Theseus, if he lived, may have lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, or perhaps as a king in the 8th or 9th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In Athens Medea, then Aegeus's consort, recognised Theseus and tried to have him killed by sending him to capture the Marathonian Bull. When he returned, she tried to poison him, but Aegeus recognised the sandals and sword and knocked the cup from his hands. Theseus then defeated the Pallantides, the sons of Pallas, who plotted against him as rivals for the throne. He is credited with the synoikismos, the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> under Athenian rule.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to send seven youths and seven maidens as tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>, which lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a>. Theseus took the place of one of the youths, promising his father to hoist a white sail if successful. Minos's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a> gave him a ball of thread, and he killed the Minotaur and escaped. He abandoned Ariadne on Naxos, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> found and married her, and forgot the white sail. Aegeus, thinking his son dead, threw himself into the sea. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripides">Euripides</a>'s version Theseus cursed his own son Hippolytus after Phaedra's false accusation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In one account Lycomedes of Skyros threw Theseus off a cliff after Theseus had lost popularity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. In 475 BCE, following an oracle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> of Athens conquered Skyros and identified a coffin holding a large corpse with a bronze spearhead and a sword as Theseus's remains, which were reburied in Athens. The Athenians preserved the thirty-oared ship in which Theseus returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, replacing its planks as they decayed, and sent it each year to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>; philosophers asked whether it remained the same ship, the Ship of Theseus paradox.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theseus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the following centuries the villages of Attica, an area of some 2,500 square kilometres, were drawn into a single state centred on Athens, a process the Athenians credited to the hero <b>Theseus</b> and called the synoikismos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The myths of Minos, the lawgiver king of Knossos, of Daedalus and the Labyrinth, of <b>Theseus</b>, Ariadne and the Minotaur, circulated throughout the Greek world, and the city deliberately advertised them on its coinage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond the Olympians, the Greek world was full of divine powers: nymphs of springs and trees, river gods, the Fates, the Furies, and heroes, mortals of the distant past such as Heracles, <b>Theseus</b> and Achilles, who were worshipped at their tombs and could help or harm the living.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greeks remembered Bronze Age Crete in myth: Minos the lawgiver king, the Labyrinth built by Daedalus, and the Minotaur killed by <b>Theseus</b> with the help of Ariadne.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Myth made it the birthplace of <b>Theseus</b> and the home of his grandfather Pittheus.</blockquote><a href="/state/troezen">Troezen</a></li></ul></section>
